WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.443
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.743 --> 00:00:09.338
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.638 --> 00:00:16.401
Unit two: Social Psychology. Today you will describe hidden influences on behaviour with the passive.

4
00:00:16.701 --> 00:00:19.458
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.865
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:24.717 --> 00:00:25.695
Conformity.

7
00:00:26.683 --> 00:00:29.937
Conformity means adjusting behaviour to match a group.

8
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:33.687
Asch's experiments revealed striking conformity.

9
00:00:34.283 --> 00:00:36.901
Your turn. Say: Conformity.

10
00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:40.849
Conformity.

11
00:00:41.583 --> 00:00:42.492
Groupthink.

12
00:00:43.483 --> 00:00:47.224
Groupthink means poor decisions caused by a group's desire for agreement.

13
00:00:47.817 --> 00:00:50.501
Groupthink led the committee to ignore the risks.

14
00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:53.757
Your turn. Say: Groupthink.

15
00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:57.404
Groupthink.

16
00:00:58.150 --> 00:00:59.836
Cognitive dissonance.

17
00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:05.369
Cognitive dissonance means discomfort from holding conflicting beliefs or actions.

18
00:01:05.983 --> 00:01:09.121
Smokers often experience cognitive dissonance.

19
00:01:09.717 --> 00:01:13.402
Your turn. Say: Cognitive dissonance.

20
00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:17.941
Cognitive dissonance.

21
00:01:18.683 --> 00:01:20.102
Social influence.

22
00:01:21.117 --> 00:01:25.229
Social influence means the effect others have on our thoughts and actions.

23
00:01:25.817 --> 00:01:29.128
Social influence is strongest among close friends.

24
00:01:29.717 --> 00:01:32.694
Your turn. Say: Social influence.

25
00:01:35.533 --> 00:01:37.544
Social influence.

26
00:01:38.283 --> 00:01:39.633
In-group bias.

27
00:01:40.617 --> 00:01:43.742
In-group bias means favouring members of one's own group.

28
00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:47.545
In-group bias appears even in randomly formed teams.

29
00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.837
Your turn. Say: In-group bias.

30
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.015
In-group bias.

31
00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:57.134
Attribution error.

32
00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:03.040
Attribution error means blaming character rather than circumstances for others' behaviour.

33
00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.776
Calling a late driver 'careless' may be an attribution error.

34
00:02:07.383 --> 00:02:10.837
Your turn. Say: Attribution error.

35
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.258
Attribution error.

36
00:02:16.017 --> 00:02:17.029
Persuasion.

37
00:02:18.017 --> 00:02:21.154
Persuasion means the process of changing attitudes.

38
00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.574
Repetition increases the power of persuasion.

39
00:02:25.183 --> 00:02:27.870
Your turn. Say: Persuasion.

40
00:02:30.733 --> 00:02:31.804
Persuasion.

41
00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:33.714
Self-perception.

42
00:02:34.717 --> 00:02:37.958
Self-perception means how people understand themselves.

43
00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:40.851
Volunteering can change self-perception.

44
00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:44.207
Your turn. Say: Self-perception.

45
00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:48.567
Self-perception.

46
00:02:49.317 --> 00:02:50.643
Heuristic bias.

47
00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:55.124
Heuristic bias means errors caused by mental shortcuts.

48
00:02:55.717 --> 00:02:58.413
Heuristic bias makes rare dangers seem common.

49
00:02:59.017 --> 00:03:01.959
Your turn. Say: Heuristic bias.

50
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.277
Heuristic bias.

51
00:03:07.017 --> 00:03:07.762
Norm.

52
00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:11.609
Norm means an unwritten rule of expected behaviour.

53
00:03:12.217 --> 00:03:14.274
Queuing is a strong social norm.

54
00:03:14.883 --> 00:03:17.234
Your turn. Say: Norm.

55
00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:20.973
Norm.

56
00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:25.696
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:32.674
Visal, your experiment asked students to judge line lengths after hearing others' answers. What happened?

58
00:03:33.783 --> 00:03:42.949
Many gave the wrong answer when the group did. Their judgement was clearly shaped by social influence rather than by what they saw.

59
00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:45.295
Did they realise it?

60
00:03:46.383 --> 00:03:55.839
Most didn't. Behaviour is often shaped by unconscious social influence, so people believe they are thinking independently.

61
00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:59.623
How could groupthink be prevented in real committees?

62
00:04:00.717 --> 00:04:09.311
Dissent should be encouraged, and one member can be assigned to argue the opposite view, so the norm of agreement is weakened.

63
00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:11.909
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.597
Grammar focus: Passive voice for behavior.

65
00:04:15.897 --> 00:04:17.142
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:21.498
Behavior is often shaped by unconscious social influence.

67
00:04:22.383 --> 00:04:25.300
Their judgement was influenced by the group's answers.

68
00:04:26.217 --> 00:04:28.681
Dissent should be encouraged in every committee.

69
00:04:29.633 --> 00:04:39.844
Social scientists use the passive to focus on the person affected and the invisible forces acting on them. Adverbs like often, largely and unconsciously go between 'be' and the participle.

70
00:04:40.583 --> 00:04:44.954
Be careful! Don't say: Behaviour is often shape by others.

71
00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:50.130
Say: Behaviour is often shaped by others.

72
00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.511
Passive needs the past participle.

73
00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:56.045
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:04:56.833 --> 00:04:58.043
The Power of the Group.

75
00:04:58.343 --> 00:05:04.882
Social psychology investigates how individuals are influenced by the real or imagined presence of others.

76
00:05:05.867 --> 00:05:13.079
In Solomon Asch's famous experiments, participants were persuaded to give obviously wrong answers simply because the group did.

77
00:05:14.067 --> 00:05:19.839
Such conformity is not necessarily weakness; it is often driven by a deep need to belong.

78
00:05:20.833 --> 00:05:25.851
However, when dissent is discouraged, groups may fall into groupthink and make disastrous decisions.

79
00:05:26.867 --> 00:05:31.641
Understanding these pressures allows us to recognise when our choices are being shaped by others.

80
00:05:32.733 --> 00:05:36.619
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:37.483 --> 00:05:40.191
Our choices are often blank by people around us.

82
00:05:40.491 --> 00:05:44.412
A, influence. B, influenced. C, influencing.

83
00:05:47.967 --> 00:05:50.291
The answer is B: influenced.

84
00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:53.891
Favouring members of your own team is blank.

85
00:05:54.191 --> 00:05:58.529
A, in-group bias. B, self-perception. C, persuasion.

86
00:06:02.067 --> 00:06:04.670
The answer is A: in-group bias.

87
00:06:05.817 --> 00:06:08.641
Discomfort from conflicting beliefs is called blank.

88
00:06:08.941 --> 00:06:13.302
A, groupthink. B, a norm. C, cognitive dissonance.

89
00:06:16.867 --> 00:06:19.702
The answer is C: cognitive dissonance.

90
00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:22.803
Which collocation is correct?

91
00:06:23.103 --> 00:06:28.603
A, give in to peer pressure. B, give up to peer pressure. C, give on peer pressure.

92
00:06:32.167 --> 00:06:35.130
The answer is A: give in to peer pressure.

93
00:06:36.283 --> 00:06:39.630
According to the reading, conformity is often driven by…

94
00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:44.129
A, a need to belong. B, weakness. C, low intelligence.

95
00:06:47.667 --> 00:06:49.759
The answer is A: a need to belong.

96
00:06:50.967 --> 00:06:53.979
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:06:54.817 --> 00:06:57.768
How does group pressure influence individual behavior?

98
00:07:14.583 --> 00:07:15.991
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:16.291 --> 00:07:52.341
Group pressure influences individual behaviour by making people doubt their own judgement and follow the majority. Our decisions are often shaped by a strong need to belong, even when we are not aware of it. For example, students may laugh at a joke they find unkind simply because everyone else does. Some argue that conformity helps societies function smoothly, which is partly true. However, I think we should be taught to recognise group pressure, so that we can resist it when it is wrong.

100
00:07:52.641 --> 00:07:54.502
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:07:55.417 --> 00:08:00.031
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and passive voice for behavior.

102
00:08:00.331 --> 00:08:05.050
Today's value is self-awareness. Know your strengths and keep growing.

103
00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:07.851
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

104
00:08:08.151 --> 00:08:12.150
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

